Here’s what was implemented.

### Approach

Next.js recommends [`after()`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/after) from `next/server` to run work **after** the response is finished, so analytics does not delay HTML.

Because the app route was otherwise **static**, the server component would only run at build time. Calling [`connection()`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/connection) first opts this page into **per-request** rendering so each visit schedules a real `after()` task, not a one-off at build.

### Files

**Server component** (`app/page.tsx`): welcome UI, `await connection()`, then `after(() => …)` for logging.

```1:25:/vercel/sandbox/app/page.tsx
import { after, connection } from 'next/server'
import { logPageView } from '@/lib/analytics'

export default async function Home() {
  // Ensure this runs per real request (not only at static build time).
  await connection()

  after(async () => {
    try {
      await logPageView({
        path: '/',
        occurredAt: new Date().toISOString(),
      })
    } catch (err) {
      console.error('[analytics] page view failed', err)
    }
  })

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Welcome</h1>
      <p>Thanks for visiting.</p>
    </main>
  )
}
```

**Analytics helper** (`lib/analytics.ts`): POST JSON to `ANALYTICS_INGEST_URL` when set; no network call if unset (safe for local dev).

Set your ingest endpoint in the environment, for example:

`ANALYTICS_INGEST_URL=https://your-analytics.example.com/v1/events`

`npm run build` succeeds and `/` is reported as **dynamic** (ƒ), so logging is tied to requests, not a single static generation pass.

